from common import unit,rule

unit('Experiencias, hábitos y cambios','Looking back, moving forward','🧭','Contar una experiencia con una cronología clara y distinguir resultados, duración y hábitos.',[
rule('El punto desde el que miras','El tiempo verbal expresa tu perspectiva. Past simple sitúa un hecho en un periodo terminado; present perfect conecta el pasado con una situación actual. Present perfect continuous destaca una actividad y su duración, sin asegurar que haya terminado. Compara un informe acabado con el trabajo que te ha tenido ocupado. Los verbos de estado, como know u own, normalmente no usan forma continua en ese sentido. Una duración no obliga al continuo: puedes decir I have known her for years. Primero decide qué quieres comunicar y después elige la forma.','finished time → past simple; current result → have + participle; ongoing activity → have been + -ing','No combines present perfect con yesterday si ese adverbio sitúa el hecho terminado.',
'I {have been working} on this report all morning, and it is still unfinished.|worked yesterday|am work',
'We {published} the results last Friday.|have published|have been publishing',
'I have known my mentor for several years.'),
rule('Una historia tiene capas','Past simple lleva los acontecimientos principales. Past continuous presenta el fondo o una actividad en marcha. Past perfect marca un hecho anterior a otro momento pasado; no hace falta mantenerlo cuando el orden ya está claro. Past perfect continuous añade duración anterior y puede explicar un resultado visible. En una narración, evita saltar de tiempo sin cambiar el punto de referencia. Dibuja una línea temporal de tres hechos, cuenta primero la escena y revela después lo que había pasado antes.','was/were + -ing; had + participle; had been + -ing','No uses past perfect solo porque un hecho ocurrió hace mucho tiempo.',
'When I reached the station, the train {had already left}.|has already left|will already leave',
'They were exhausted because they {had been walking} for hours before they found shelter.|have been walking|will walk',
'I was checking the map when the battery died.'),
rule('Hábitos que ya no son iguales','Used to sirve para hábitos y estados pasados que contrastan con el presente. Would puede contar acciones repetidas cuando el periodo pasado está establecido, pero normalmente no estados como own o believe. Be used to significa estar acostumbrado; get used to describe el proceso de adaptación. En estas dos estructuras to es preposición: detrás va un nombre o -ing. Cuenta un cambio real, explica qué costó y cómo te adaptaste; así practicas significado y forma juntos.','used to + base; be/get used to + noun/-ing','Did you use to…? y I did not use to… llevan use, sin -d.',
'I am used to {working} with people in different time zones.|work|worked',
'We {used to own} a small shop, but we sold it.|would own|are used to own',
'During those summers, we would meet by the river every evening.')],
'''setback|contratiempo|The delay was a setback, but we found another route.
milestone|hito|Finishing the first draft was an important milestone.
adapt to|adaptarse a|It took me weeks to adapt to the new timetable.
make an effort|esforzarse|Please make an effort to explain your reasoning.
keep track of|llevar un registro de|I keep track of the expressions I can use confidently.
carry on|continuar|We decided to carry on despite the noise.
look back on|recordar al mirar atrás|I look back on that journey with affection.
take for granted|dar por sentado|We sometimes take reliable transport for granted.
approach|enfoque|Their approach combines observation with regular practice.
outcome|resultado|The outcome was better than we had expected.
awareness|conciencia de algo|The campaign raised awareness of local history.
gradually|gradualmente|Our conversations gradually became more natural.
eventually|finalmente, tras un tiempo|We eventually found a room we could afford.
previously|anteriormente|I had previously worked in a much smaller team.
at first|al principio|At first, I was reluctant to ask questions.
meanwhile|mientras tanto|The teacher checked the equipment; meanwhile, we prepared our notes.
long-term|a largo plazo|We need a long-term plan rather than a quick solution.
reliable|fiable|A reliable partner tells you when something goes wrong.
resilient|capaz de recuperarse|The group became more resilient after the difficult season.
achievement|logro|Completing the interview without a script was a personal achievement.''',
'''When Mara moved to another city, she expected her English to improve simply because she would hear it every day. She had studied grammar for years and could explain several complicated rules. Yet her first week at a community workshop was uncomfortable. While other volunteers were discussing the schedule, she was silently translating each sentence. By the time she had prepared a reply, the conversation had moved on.

The organiser suggested a smaller goal: ask one useful question at every meeting and report one decision afterwards. Mara began keeping a notebook, but she did not fill it with isolated words. Instead, she recorded expressions she had needed, such as asking someone to clarify a deadline. The next week she deliberately used those expressions in a different situation.

Three months later, Mara still occasionally misses a joke. However, she has become much better at noticing when she needs clarification. She has been helping new volunteers this month, which gives her a reason to explain procedures without reading. She used to judge a conversation by the number of mistakes she made. Now she also asks whether the people involved understood each other and completed their task. Her progress has not been a straight line, but it has become easier to recognise.''',
'''What initially prevented Mara from joining discussions?|She was preparing translations while the conversation continued.|She could not read the schedule.~The other volunteers refused to speak to her.|El primer párrafo explica el desfase entre preparar una respuesta y usarla.
Why did she record expressions instead of isolated words?|She wanted language she could reuse to accomplish a task.|She wanted a longer notebook.~The organiser prohibited vocabulary lists.|El cuaderno parte de necesidades comunicativas concretas.
What does the final paragraph suggest about her progress?|She now measures success partly by successful communication.|She never misses any information.~She has stopped caring about clarity.|El criterio cambia: comprenderse y completar la tarea.
Which event happened before the first workshop?|She had studied grammar for years.|She began helping new volunteers.~She adopted the organiser's smaller goal.|Had studied sitúa el estudio antes de la primera experiencia.''',
'''At an evening class, two learners compared their study records. Joel had completed ninety short exercises in one weekend. Lina had completed fewer, but she could describe three situations in which she had reused the language. Joel felt disappointed until their tutor asked him to tell a story about a missed train. He knew the vocabulary, yet he kept changing between present and past without a reason.

They tried the task again with three notes: background, interruption and earlier cause. This time, Joel explained that he had been looking at the wrong platform while an announcement was being made. Lina listened and asked what he had done next. Her unexpected question made him retrieve the language rather than repeat a script.

A week later, both learners told a new story without the notes. Joel still hesitated, but his listener could follow the events. They agreed to keep the exercise totals in their records while adding a second measure: something they could now do outside the exercise. Neither learner treated one good performance as proof that the skill would always be available.''',
'''What was missing from Joel's original record?|Evidence of using the language in a new situation.|The exact number of exercises.~A list of every train station.|Cantidad de ejercicios no equivale a transferencia.
Why did the three notes help?|They gave his story a clear time structure.|They supplied every word of a script.~They removed the need for a listener.|Las notas organizan fondo, interrupción y causa anterior.
What made Lina's question useful?|Joel had to produce an unprepared response.|Joel could copy an answer.~Lina answered the question herself.|La pregunta inesperada exige recuperar lenguaje.
What attitude do they adopt at the end?|They will look for repeated evidence of practical ability.|They assume one successful attempt proves permanent mastery.~They stop recording practice.|El último enunciado rechaza sacar conclusiones de una sola actuación.''',
'''Welcome to the volunteer meeting. Before we choose this month's tasks, I want to explain a change. We used to send a long message on Sunday, but several people missed updates that arrived later. For the past two weeks, we have been keeping a shared list instead. You do not need to read the whole history: check the date beside each task and ask the person responsible if anything is unclear. Yesterday, a delivery arrived late because the driver had received an old address. That incident showed us why confirming information matters. After today's meeting, please tell your partner one decision and one question you still have. We will compare these notes before anyone leaves.''',
'''Why did the group change its system?|People missed updates sent after the weekly message.|Nobody could read English.~Sunday meetings were cancelled.|El problema era el cambio de información después del mensaje.
Why was the delivery late?|The driver had an outdated address.|The shared list was deleted.~The meeting finished early.|La causa es anterior a la entrega.
What should each person report after the meeting?|One decision and one remaining question.|A complete translation of all messages.~Only the date of the next meeting.|Se piden dos elementos específicos.''',
'Escribe 140–190 palabras para un compañero que empieza un voluntariado. Cuenta una dificultad real o inventada, qué había ocurrido antes y cómo ha cambiado tu forma de actuar. Incluye un consejo justificado.',
'''When I joined a community garden, I assumed that understanding the instructions would be enough. I had studied English at school, but I had rarely used it while doing a practical task. During my first session, I kept agreeing even when I was unsure what people meant. As a result, I planted several seedlings in the wrong place.

Since then, I have been trying a different approach. I repeat the important part of an instruction in my own words and ask whether I have understood correctly. At first, I felt that this would annoy the other volunteers. In fact, it has prevented several misunderstandings and helped us work together.

My advice is to choose one small communication goal for each session. You could ask a follow-up question or explain a procedure to someone new. Keep a note of what worked, then try it again in another situation. Progress becomes more visible when you notice what you can actually do.''',
'Cuenta durante dos minutos una adaptación difícil. Tu compañero debe interrumpir con dos preguntas. Después resume su experiencia sin confundir la cronología.',
'''I started working here three years ago and I still work here. Complete: I ___ here for three years.|have worked~have been working|Ambas formas conectan el inicio pasado con el presente; el continuo enfatiza la actividad.
She no longer owns a bicycle. Complete: She ___ a bicycle.|used to own|Used to puede describir un estado pasado.
Working at night is familiar to me. Complete: I am ___ at night.|used to working|Tras be used to usamos -ing.
The meeting ended before we arrived. Complete: When we arrived, the meeting ___.|had ended~had already ended|Past perfect sitúa el final antes de nuestra llegada.''',
'''Regular feedback raised our ___ of common errors.|AWARE|awareness|Tras our necesitamos un sustantivo.
Finishing the project was a major ___.|ACHIEVE|achievement|Major modifica al sustantivo achievement.
The bus service is usually ___.|RELY|reliable|Tras is se necesita el adjetivo.
We noticed a gradual ___ in clarity.|IMPROVE|improvement|A gradual introduce un sustantivo contable.''',
'Cronología oral: divide tu historia en grupos de sentido y destaca el hecho que cambió la situación. No aceleres todas las palabras por igual.')

unit('Planes, previsiones y plazos','Planning ahead','📅','Explicar previsiones y compromisos, negociar plazos y distinguir procesos futuros de resultados completados.',[
rule('Elegir una forma de futuro','Will puede expresar una decisión al hablar, una promesa o una predicción. Going to suele presentar una intención previa o una predicción basada en evidencia actual. Present continuous puede presentar un acuerdo concreto; present simple sirve para horarios. Estas formas se solapan según el contexto: una palabra como tomorrow no determina por sí sola la elección. Al planificar, aclara qué está decidido, qué depende de otras personas y qué solo es una posibilidad. Practica una conversación en la que alguien cambia el plan y debes responder.','decision now: will; intention/evidence: going to; arrangement: present continuous','Una previsión no se convierte en compromiso solo porque uses will.',
'The train {leaves} at seven according to the timetable.|will leaving|is leave',
'Those boxes are unstable. They {are going to fall}.|are go to fall|have fallen tomorrow',
'I am meeting the designer on Thursday; we have already agreed on the time.'),
rule('Estar haciendo frente a haber terminado','Future continuous coloca una actividad en desarrollo en un momento futuro. Future perfect mira hacia atrás desde un punto futuro y presenta algo ya terminado. By significa como máximo para ese momento; until marca continuidad hasta un límite. Future perfect continuous añade duración hasta ese punto, sobre todo con actividades. Elige primero una fecha de referencia y después pregunta: ¿la tarea estará en marcha, completada o habrá durado cierto tiempo? No conviertas todas las fechas límite en until.','will be + -ing; will have + participle; will have been + -ing','By Friday y until Friday expresan relaciones temporales diferentes.',
'By Friday, we {will have completed} the first stage.|will completing|have complete',
'At ten tomorrow, I {will be presenting} the proposal.|will have present|am present',
'By June, we will have been working together for a year.'),
rule('Condiciones y calendarios realistas','En una cláusula temporal que se refiere al futuro, after, before, once, when y as soon as suelen llevar presente, no will. El present perfect puede destacar que una acción estará completada antes de la siguiente. Unless significa if not; provided that introduce una condición necesaria. In case suele presentar una precaución ante una posibilidad y no equivale simplemente a if. Para negociar un plazo, combina un compromiso limitado, una condición y un plan alternativo.','once/as soon as + present; main clause + future','I will call when I arrive, sin will después de when en esta cláusula temporal.',
'We will send the invitation once everyone {has confirmed}.|will confirm|will have confirm',
'Take a printed copy in case the network {fails}.|will fail|would failed',
'We can finish by Monday provided that the materials arrive on time.')],
'''deadline|fecha límite|We need a realistic deadline for the first version.
schedule|calendario o programa|The schedule includes time for testing.
commitment|compromiso|Please tell us if you cannot meet that commitment.
forecast|previsión|The forecast may change as new information arrives.
postpone|aplazar|We may need to postpone the outdoor session.
bring forward|adelantar una fecha|Can we bring forward the review meeting?
set aside|reservar|Set aside half an hour to check the figures.
on track|según lo previsto|The project is still on track despite the delay.
ahead of schedule|antes de lo previsto|The repairs were finished ahead of schedule.
backup plan|plan alternativo|Our backup plan is to meet online.
be due to|tener previsto|The new service is due to start in May.
be likely to|ser probable que|Demand is likely to increase during the holidays.
available|disponible|Is a smaller room available on Tuesday?
feasible|viable|A one-week trial seems feasible with the current team.
priority|prioridad|Safety is our first priority during the event.
allocate|asignar|We should allocate more time to questions.
estimate|estimar|It is difficult to estimate how long the repair will take.
confirm|confirmar|Could you confirm the final number of guests?
in advance|con antelación|Please book your place in advance.
contingency|posible imprevisto previsto en un plan|The budget includes a small amount for a contingency.''',
'''A student association decided to organise a skills fair. At the first meeting, everyone spoke confidently about what would happen, but the calendar soon revealed a problem: three people had promised the same room to different speakers. The organiser, Nia, asked the team to separate intentions from confirmed arrangements.

They created three columns. One contained bookings that had already been agreed. Another contained tasks they expected to finish by a particular date. The third listed events that depended on something else. By the end of the week, they would have contacted every speaker, but they would not publish the programme until the room bookings were confirmed.

One volunteer proposed printing hundreds of programmes immediately to save money. Another pointed out that a cheap early order could become expensive if the schedule changed. They decided to print a small number and display a digital version that could be updated. A paper notice would remain available in case the network failed.

The fair eventually started fifteen minutes late because a visiting speaker missed a train. This was inconvenient, but it did not destroy the day. The team used a prepared discussion activity while they waited. Their plan had become useful precisely because it included uncertainty rather than hiding it behind confident predictions.''',
'''What was the initial planning problem?|Different people had promised the same room.|No speakers wanted to attend.~The association had no calendar.|La duplicación de compromisos aparece al principio.
What had to happen before publication?|The room bookings had to be confirmed.|Every programme had to be printed.~The fair had to begin.|Until introduce el límite anterior a publicar.
Why did they avoid a large early print order?|Changes could make those programmes unusable.|Digital versions were forbidden.~They expected no visitors.|El ahorro inicial podía generar desperdicio después.
Why was the backup activity helpful?|It gave visitors something useful to do during a delay.|It allowed the speaker to cancel permanently.~It guaranteed every train would arrive.|El plan alternativo responde a la demora real.''',
'''A neighbourhood library plans to open on Sunday mornings for a six-week trial. Volunteers have offered to help, but the librarian refuses to announce permanent opening hours yet. By the final week, the library will have collected attendance figures and comments from visitors. During the trial, staff will be comparing the demand with the cost of keeping the building open.

Several residents would like a decision immediately. The librarian explains that making a promise before evaluating the trial could lead to a sudden closure later. Instead, the notice gives a clear review date and states which services will be available. Book borrowing will continue as usual, whereas technical support will depend on a specialist being present.

The plan includes a practical precaution: if fewer than two volunteers can attend, the session will be cancelled and a notice will be posted the previous evening. This condition is visible before anyone registers. Residents may dislike the uncertainty, but they can make better plans when it is expressed honestly.''',
'''What is the purpose of the trial?|To gather evidence before making a long-term commitment.|To close the library permanently.~To remove all borrowing services.|El ensayo sirve para valorar demanda y coste.
What will staff be doing during the trial?|Comparing demand with operating costs.|Promising unlimited technical support.~Publishing a permanent timetable.|El continuo presenta una actividad en marcha.
What depends on a specialist attending?|Technical support.|All borrowing.~The review date.|El texto distingue el servicio habitual del condicional.
Why is the cancellation condition published?|So residents can plan with accurate information.|So nobody can register.~So volunteers never need to attend.|La condición visible comunica límites reales.''',
'''Here is the revised plan for Saturday's workshop. At nine, the technicians will be setting up the equipment, so participants should arrive at half past nine. By ten, we expect to have checked every device. The main activity will begin as soon as those checks are complete. We originally intended to use the courtyard, but the weather forecast is uncertain, and we have now booked the upstairs room. Please bring headphones in case you need to work independently. If you cannot attend, tell us by Thursday rather than on the morning itself. We will confirm the final groups on Friday. The end time remains one o'clock, although the order of the activities may change.''',
'''When should participants arrive?|At half past nine.|At nine.~At one.|Las nueve son para preparación técnica.
Why has the location changed?|The weather is uncertain.|The upstairs room is closed.~The courtyard has no chairs.|La previsión motiva reservar interior.
What remains unchanged?|The finishing time.|The order of activities.~The group membership.|La última frase fija el final a la una.''',
'Redacta un correo de 140–190 palabras para organizar un taller. Distingue tres acuerdos confirmados de dos previsiones, incluye un plazo, una condición y una alternativa.',
'''Hi everyone,

I am writing to confirm the arrangements for our photography workshop. We have booked the community hall for Saturday, and two local photographers have agreed to lead the practical sessions. Participants will arrive at ten, while the organising team will be setting up from nine.

By Thursday, we will have received the final list of equipment. Please let me know before then if you can lend us a tripod. We expect most people to bring a phone, so the activities will not require an expensive camera.

We are planning a short walk after lunch, provided that the weather is suitable. If it rains, we will practise indoor portraits instead. Please bring a light jacket in case the temperature changes during the walk.

I will send the group list as soon as everyone has confirmed. Could you check these arrangements and tell me if I have missed anything? It would be useful to resolve any problems before Friday.

Best wishes,
Rosa''',
'Negocia un evento con presupuesto y tiempo limitados. Tu compañero cambia una condición a mitad de la conversación. Resume qué queda confirmado y qué sigue siendo una previsión.',
'''The task will be finished before Friday. Complete: By Friday, we ___ the task.|will have finished|Miramos un resultado completado desde un punto futuro.
We cannot publish before they agree. Complete: We will publish as soon as they ___.|agree~have agreed|La cláusula temporal usa presente o present perfect.
Perhaps the network will fail, so take a copy. Complete: Take a copy ___ the network fails.|in case|Es una precaución ante una posibilidad.
We will only start if the room is ready. Complete: We will start provided ___ ready.|that the room is~the room is|Provided (that) introduce la condición.''',
'''We need written ___ of the booking.|CONFIRM|confirmation|Written modifica un nombre.
The plan is realistic and ___.|ACHIEVE|achievable|Se necesita un adjetivo que exprese posibilidad.
Their ___ may depend on the weather.|ARRIVE|arrival|Their introduce un sustantivo.
There is some ___ about the date.|CERTAIN|uncertainty|Some acompaña al sustantivo; el sentido exige el prefijo negativo.''',
'Fechas y límites: destaca by, until y once; confirma una fecha repitiéndola con una pregunta natural, sin convertir cada frase en una lista de palabras.')

unit('Decisiones y alternativas','What might have happened','🔀','Analizar decisiones pasadas y sus consecuencias actuales sin confundir hechos con hipótesis.',[
rule('Un pasado que no ocurrió','Third conditional imagina una condición pasada diferente y su resultado pasado. La realidad se deduce del contexto: If we had left earlier sugiere que no salimos antes. Had + participle va en la condición; would have + participle expresa el resultado imaginado. Could have añade posibilidad o capacidad; might have expresa un resultado menos seguro. Antes de formar la frase, escribe dos hechos reales y luego cambia uno mentalmente. La contracción had y would puede sonar igual, pero su posición y el verbo siguiente aclaran su función.','if + had + participle → would/could/might have + participle','En este patrón evita would have en la cláusula con if.',
'If we {had checked} the address, we would have found the building.|would have checked|have checked',
'She would have attended if she {had received} the invitation.|would receive|has received',
'Without your advice, I might have made the same mistake.'),
rule('Cuando el resultado llega hasta hoy','Un mixed conditional puede unir una condición pasada irreal con un resultado presente: una decisión anterior explica una situación imaginada ahora. También puede unir una característica presente con una consecuencia pasada. Las formas no se eligen por una receta única; se eligen según el momento de cada idea. Añade now o yesterday cuando ayuden al interlocutor, pero no dependas solo de esas palabras. Explica primero la realidad y después la alternativa para que no parezca que estás afirmando algo que ocurrió.','past cause → if + had done, would be now; present trait → if + past, would have done','Identifica por separado el tiempo de la condición y el del resultado.',
'If I had accepted that job, I {would live} abroad now.|would have lived yesterday|will live',
'If I were more confident, I {would have spoken} at yesterday\'s meeting.|will have spoken|had spoken',
'If they had repaired the bridge, the road would be open now.'),
rule('Alternativas con límites y matices','Unless introduce una condición negativa. Provided that, as long as y on condition that presentan límites o requisitos. Even if indica que el resultado se mantiene a pesar de una posibilidad. Supposing invita a explorar una hipótesis. Al discutir una decisión, compara opciones y consecuencias en vez de formar frases aisladas: qué se habría evitado, qué sigue siendo incierto y qué harías de otra manera. Una hipótesis razonable debe respetar los hechos conocidos y no presentar una predicción como certeza.','unless = if not; even if = aunque ocurra; provided that = siempre que','Unless no suele combinarse con otra negación para el mismo sentido.',
'We can use the hall {provided that} we leave it clean.|despite|unless that',
'{Even if} the price falls, we will not buy it because we do not need it.|Unless|Provided',
'Supposing you had missed the last bus, what would you have done?')],
'''consequence|consecuencia|Every decision can have an unexpected consequence.
alternative|alternativa|We considered a cheaper alternative.
regret|lamentar|I regret not asking for clarification.
take a risk|arriesgarse|Would you take a risk to learn something new?
avoid|evitar|A clearer message could avoid confusion.
prevent|impedir|An earlier warning might prevent another delay.
turn down|rechazar una oferta|She decided to turn down the job offer.
miss out on|perderse una oportunidad|I do not want to miss out on the discussion.
weigh up|sopesar|We need to weigh up the advantages and disadvantages.
in hindsight|en retrospectiva|In hindsight, we should have tested the plan.
otherwise|de lo contrario|Check the address; otherwise, you may get lost.
provided that|siempre que|You can borrow it provided that you return it tomorrow.
even if|incluso si|I would try again even if I failed.
assumption|suposición|That assumption may be incorrect.
evidence|pruebas o indicios|We need evidence before drawing a conclusion.
reasonable|razonable|That sounds like a reasonable explanation.
unlikely|poco probable|A complete cancellation is unlikely.
opportunity|oportunidad|The course gave her an opportunity to lead a team.
responsibility|responsabilidad|We share responsibility for checking the details.
trade-off|equilibrio entre ventajas y desventajas|There is a trade-off between cost and flexibility.''',
'''A small theatre had to choose between repairing its old lights and hiring new equipment for one season. The director preferred hiring because it required less money immediately. A technician warned that the company would still need a permanent solution later, but the first performance was approaching and the decision was made quickly.

The hired lights worked well. However, when the following season began, prices had risen and the theatre could no longer afford the same arrangement. Some volunteers argued that repairing the original equipment would certainly have solved everything. The technician disagreed with that certainty: the old lights might have needed further repairs, and the earlier inspection had not covered every part.

At a review meeting, the team separated what they knew from what they could only imagine. If they had requested a detailed inspection, they would have had better information. That did not prove that repairing was the best choice, but it revealed a weakness in the decision process. They agreed that future decisions would include an estimate of costs over several seasons, even if a short-term option initially looked attractive. The discussion became more useful when the question changed from who was to blame to what information they had failed to obtain.''',
'''Why did the director choose hiring?|It required less money at the time.|The old lights had been fully inspected.~Hiring was guaranteed to remain cheap.|La razón era el coste inmediato.
Why did the technician question the volunteers' certainty?|The old equipment could have needed further repairs.|She knew repairs would cost nothing.~The hired lights had never worked.|La alternativa también era incierta.
What would an inspection have provided?|Better information for the decision.|A guarantee of future prices.~An extra theatre building.|Información no equivale a garantía.
What lesson does the team take from the meeting?|Consider longer-term costs and missing evidence.|Always choose the most expensive equipment.~Never hire equipment.|Se mejora el proceso de decisión, sin regla absoluta.''',
'''Leo declined an exchange programme because he assumed his application would be unsuccessful. Months later, a friend explained that the organisers also considered motivation and practical experience. Leo realised he had rejected himself before anyone else could assess him.

He cannot know whether he would have been accepted. Saying that he would certainly be living abroad now would ignore that uncertainty. He can, however, identify a different decision he could have made: ask for information and apply. If he had applied, he might at least have gained experience of the process.

For the next opportunity, Leo plans to list the evidence for each concern. A genuine financial obstacle needs a different response from a fear based on an assumption. He will apply provided that the travel costs are covered. Even if he is not selected, he expects the experience to help him prepare a stronger application later.''',
'''What prevented Leo from applying?|An assumption about being rejected.|A confirmed rejection.~A completed assessment of his skills.|No llegó a presentar solicitud.
Which outcome is uncertain?|Whether he would have been accepted.|Whether he declined to apply.~Whether he later spoke to his friend.|El resultado alternativo no se conoce.
What condition does he set for applying next time?|Travel costs must be covered.|Selection must be guaranteed.~His friend must apply too.|Provided that presenta el requisito.
What value might rejection still have?|It could provide useful application experience.|It would prove he has no ability.~It would remove every future obstacle.|Distingue experiencia útil de resultado deseado.''',
'''Looking back at our field trip, I think we made one avoidable mistake. We checked the weather, but we did not ask whether the visitor centre would be open. If we had called ahead, we would have known that it was closed for repairs. That would not necessarily have stopped us going; we might have chosen a different meeting point. Next time, we will confirm opening hours as well as transport. We can still organise an outdoor activity provided that there is a covered alternative. Even if the forecast is good, we should have a plan for unexpected rain. Please suggest one improvement based on evidence, rather than simply blaming the person who booked the bus.''',
'''What information was not checked?|Whether the visitor centre would be open.|The weather forecast.~The bus booking.|Se comprobó clima, no apertura.
What might they have changed?|The meeting point.|The entire language course.~The ownership of the centre.|El hablante no afirma que cancelarían necesariamente.
What kind of suggestion is requested?|An improvement supported by evidence.|An accusation against the organiser.~A guaranteed weather prediction.|La propuesta debe basarse en lo aprendido.''',
'Escribe 140–190 palabras sobre una decisión. Expón los hechos, dos alternativas, una consecuencia presente y una mejora para la próxima vez. No presentes resultados hipotéticos como hechos.',
'''Our group chose a crowded café for an online interview because it was close to the station. We had checked the connection, but we had not considered how noisy the room might become. During the interview, we had to ask several questions again, which made the conversation difficult.

If we had visited at the same time of day beforehand, we would have noticed the problem. We could also have booked a quiet room at the library. That option would have required more planning, but it might have produced a clearer recording.

The experience still affects our work: we now keep a checklist for interviews. If we had ignored the problem, we would probably still be choosing places based only on convenience. Next time, we will test both sound and connection, even if the venue has good reviews. We cannot prevent every interruption, but we can prepare an alternative and explain any limitations honestly to our guest.''',
'Debatan una decisión con información incompleta. Uno defiende una alternativa y el otro señala incertidumbres. Terminen con una mejora verificable para el futuro.',
'''We did not leave early, so we missed the start. Complete: If we had left early, we ___ the start.|would not have missed~wouldn't have missed|Condición y resultado se sitúan en el pasado.
I did not train, and I am not fit now. Complete: If I had trained, I ___ fit now.|would be|La consecuencia es presente.
You may attend only if you register. Complete: You may attend provided ___ register.|that you~you|Provided that introduce un requisito.
The price may fall, but I still will not buy it. Complete: ___ the price falls, I will not buy it.|Even if|El resultado no cambia aunque se cumpla esa posibilidad.''',
'''We need a ___ explanation.|REASON|reasonable|El adjetivo modifica explanation.
The ___ was based on limited evidence.|DECIDE|decision|El nombre designa la decisión.
She accepted ___ for the error.|RESPONSIBLE|responsibility|Accept responsibility es una colocación.
There is a ___ that the plan will change.|POSSIBLE|possibility|A exige un sustantivo contable aquí.''',
'Contracciones en contexto: identifica would have y had mediante el verbo y la cláusula. Practica una alternativa y deja una pausa antes de su consecuencia.')

unit('Deseos, preferencias y arrepentimientos','I wish things were different','🌤️','Expresar deseos y desacuerdos con tacto, y convertir un arrepentimiento en una acción concreta.',[
rule('Desear un presente distinto','Wish + past simple expresa que te gustaría una situación presente diferente. Con be, were se usa con todos los sujetos en estilo hipotético; was también aparece en contextos menos formales. Wish + could expresa capacidad o posibilidad deseada. No interpretes el pasado de esta estructura como una fecha anterior: señala distancia respecto de la realidad. Para un futuro que consideras posible, hope suele ser más apropiado. Explica qué situación real te gustaría cambiar antes de practicar la estructura.','wish + past; wish + could; hope + present/future','I hope you enjoy it expresa una esperanza posible; no I wish you enjoy it.',
'I wish I {had} more time to practise today.|have|will have',
'I hope the interview {goes} well tomorrow.|went|had gone',
'I wish I could understand the announcements more easily.'),
rule('Mirar atrás sin quedarse allí','Wish + past perfect presenta un arrepentimiento sobre algo pasado. If only puede expresar un deseo más intenso y también usa pasado o past perfect según el momento. Should have + participle evalúa lo que habría sido mejor hacer; no afirma que se hiciera. Relaciona cada arrepentimiento con una medida concreta: pedir detalles, confirmar un horario o empezar antes. Así la práctica consiste en explicar una experiencia y aprender de ella, no en repetir lamentos sin contexto.','wish/if only + had + participle; should have + participle','I wish I had asked significa que no pregunté y ahora lo lamento.',
'I wish I {had saved} a copy before the laptop stopped working.|saved tomorrow|would save yesterday',
'We should {have checked} the instructions more carefully.|had checked|to check',
'If only we had asked for feedback sooner.'),
rule('Quejas y preferencias con tacto','Wish + would puede expresar molestia por una conducta o deseo de cambio; evita usarlo mecánicamente para tus propios estados. Would rather + verbo base expresa tu preferencia sobre tu acción. Would rather + sujeto + pasado expresa una preferencia sobre la acción de otra persona. It is time + sujeto + pasado señala que algo debería hacerse ya. El tono importa: describe el efecto de la conducta y formula una petición clara, no una acusación general.','would rather do; would rather someone did; it is time someone did','Después de would rather, la misma persona usa base verbal, sin to.',
'I would rather {discuss} this in person.|to discuss|discussing',
'I would rather you {sent} the details by email.|send yesterday|to send',
'It is time we reviewed the way we share information.')],
'''wish|desear algo distinto|I wish the room were quieter.
hope|esperar que ocurra algo|I hope your presentation goes well.
regrettable|lamentable|The misunderstanding was regrettable but avoidable.
frustrated|frustrado|She felt frustrated when nobody replied.
considerate|considerado con otros|It was considerate of you to warn us.
disturb|molestar o interrumpir|Please do not disturb the people taking the test.
put off|posponer|We cannot put off the discussion forever.
speak up|hablar para hacerse oír|It is easier to solve a problem when people speak up.
sort out|resolver|Let us sort out the timetable together.
let down|decepcionar|I did not want to let down the rest of the team.
make up for|compensar|An apology cannot always make up for lost time.
apologise for|disculparse por|I want to apologise for missing the meeting.
preference|preferencia|My preference is to work in a quiet room.
compromise|acuerdo intermedio|We reached a compromise about the schedule.
boundary|límite personal|A clear boundary helps people understand what is acceptable.
constructive|constructivo|Try to give constructive feedback with an example.
annoyance|molestia|He expressed his annoyance without raising his voice.
perspective|perspectiva|Hearing her perspective changed my reaction.
take action|tomar medidas|We need to take action before the problem grows.
move on|seguir adelante|After reviewing the mistake, we were ready to move on.''',
'''Two flatmates, Ana and Ben, had avoided discussing the noise in their shared kitchen. Ana worked early and wished the apartment were quieter at night. Ben studied late and assumed that the walls were thick enough to prevent anyone being disturbed. Both were becoming frustrated, yet neither knew the other's routine in detail.

One evening, Ana said, "I wish you would stop making so much noise." Ben heard this as criticism of his whole lifestyle. The conversation became less productive until Ana explained the specific problem: she could hear cupboard doors closing after midnight. Ben said he wished she had mentioned it earlier, because he could easily have prepared his food before studying.

They agreed on a trial arrangement rather than a permanent rule immediately. Ben would prepare what he needed before ten, and Ana would tell him if the noise continued. Ana also acknowledged that she had sometimes played music early in the morning without considering Ben's sleep.

The agreement did not require either person to become completely silent. It required each to describe a need, understand its effect on the other and test a practical change. A week later, they reviewed the arrangement and adjusted one detail. Their first uncomfortable conversation had become the start of a workable habit.''',
'''Why had the problem continued?|Neither flatmate understood the other's routine clearly.|Both wanted to move out immediately.~The kitchen had no cupboards.|El problema combina falta de información y evitación.
Why did Ben react badly at first?|He interpreted the complaint as a general criticism.|He could not hear Ana.~He had already agreed to a rule.|La formulación inicial no especificaba bien la conducta.
What made a solution easier?|Ana identified the particular noise and its timing.|They stopped discussing their needs.~Ben refused to prepare food.|El detalle permite una acción concreta.
What does the final paragraph emphasise?|Reviewing a practical change can improve cooperation.|One conversation removes all future problems.~Only Ana needed to change.|Se presenta un hábito de revisión, no perfección.''',
'''A club member regretted not challenging an unrealistic deadline. At the time, everyone sounded confident, and she did not want to appear negative. When the project ran late, she wrote that she wished she had spoken up.

Her mentor asked her to turn that sentence into a future action. Instead of promising to be braver in general, she prepared two questions: what evidence supported the estimate, and which task could be reduced if time became short? At the next meeting, these questions led to a calmer discussion than a direct claim that the plan was impossible.

The member still preferred to think before speaking. She did not need to become a different person. She needed a useful way to contribute while the decision was still open. Looking back helped because it changed what she did next.''',
'''Why did the member originally remain silent?|She did not want to seem negative.|She had no access to the meeting.~She believed the project was already finished.|Se describe una preocupación social.
What did the mentor ask her to do?|Translate regret into a specific future action.|Repeat the complaint more loudly.~Stop attending meetings.|El foco pasa del lamento a la conducta.
Why were the prepared questions effective?|They invited evidence and alternatives.|They guaranteed an earlier finish.~They avoided discussing time.|Promueven una discusión útil sin acusar.
What personal quality did she retain?|Her preference for thinking before speaking.|Her refusal to ask questions.~Her certainty that deadlines are pointless.|La mejora no exige cambiar toda la personalidad.''',
'''I would like to revisit yesterday's group discussion. I wish I had explained my concern more clearly. When I said the plan was too ambitious, I did not mean that the idea was bad. I meant that we had not allowed enough time to check the results. I would rather we tested a smaller version first. If that works, we can expand it. I also realise that I interrupted you before you had finished, and I apologise for that. Could we set aside ten minutes tomorrow to compare the two options? Please bring an estimate for each stage, so that we can discuss the time involved instead of relying on general impressions.''',
'''What does the speaker regret?|Not explaining the concern clearly.|Having no interest in the idea.~Testing a smaller version.|Wish + past perfect mira al intercambio anterior.
What does the speaker prefer?|Testing a smaller version first.|Cancelling every future project.~Expanding immediately without checks.|Would rather introduce la preferencia.
What should the listener bring?|An estimate for each stage.|A completed final report.~An apology from another person.|La petición concreta prepara la comparación.''',
'Escribe un mensaje de 140–190 palabras para resolver una dificultad compartida. Describe una conducta concreta, reconoce tu parte, expresa una preferencia y propone revisar un acuerdo.',
'''Hi Max,

Could we talk about how we organise the shared presentation? I am finding it difficult to combine our sections because I usually receive your changes shortly before the deadline. I wish we had agreed on a review date at the beginning, as that would have made things easier for both of us.

I also realise that my instructions were not very clear. I sent several separate messages, and some of them contained different versions of the same plan. I am sorry for the confusion this caused.

For the next section, I would rather we used one shared document and marked any unfinished parts. Could we aim to have a draft ready on Wednesday? That would leave Thursday for checking the examples together.

If this timing is difficult for you, please suggest an alternative. We can try the new arrangement for one week and then discuss whether it has helped. I would like us both to feel comfortable raising problems early.

Thanks,
Eva''',
'Representen un desacuerdo entre compañeros. Cada uno explica una necesidad, reconoce un punto del otro y propone una acción. Repite la conversación usando un tono más conciliador.',
'''I do not have enough time. Complete: I wish I ___ more time.|had|Pasado formal para un deseo presente.
I did not listen carefully. Complete: I wish I ___ carefully.|had listened|Past perfect para arrepentimiento pasado.
I prefer to leave now. Complete: I would rather ___ now.|leave|Verbo base tras would rather con el mismo sujeto.
Please do not call so late. Complete: I would rather you ___ so late.|did not call~didn't call|Preferencia sobre otra persona: pasado.''',
'''Thank you for your ___ during the delay.|PATIENT|patience|Your precede al sustantivo patience.
We reached a practical ___.|AGREE|agreement|Agreement es el sustantivo contable.
She expressed her ___ calmly.|ANNOY|annoyance|Se nombra la emoción.
The feedback was specific and ___.|CONSTRUCT|constructive|Adjetivo paralelo a specific.''',
'Tacto: prueba la misma petición con una pausa antes del problema y énfasis en la solución. La entonación puede suavizar una frase, pero no sustituye un contenido respetuoso.')
